<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Extremadura</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Extremadura is a region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> bordering Portugal. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos">Alarcos</a> in 1195, Almohad Caliphate forces devastated Extremadura and neighbouring regions, raiding deep into Christian territory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/extremadura.jpg" alt="Extremadura" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Extremadura_in_Spain_(including_Canarias).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The victory earned him the honorific al-Mansur, &quot;the Victorious&quot;, and in the following campaigns his forces devastated <b>Extremadura</b>, the Tagus valley and La Mancha, raiding to the walls of Toledo in the deepest Muslim penetration of Christian territory for generations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The union also brought the Kingdom of Galicia and León's frontier lands in <b>Extremadura</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Roman province of western Iberia governed from Emerita Augusta, brought Roman towns, roads, mining and the Latin language to the lands of modern Portugal and <b>Extremadura</b> for over four centuries, leaving monuments such as Mérida and Alcántara and giving Portugal the classical name it still uses for itself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It began in 1964, and its origin is uncertain: some credit Mexican engineers who had settled there, others Spaniards from <b>Extremadura</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/las-mananitas">Las Mañanitas</a></li><li><blockquote>Strabo and Pliny describe Celtici in the south-west, between the Guadiana and the Atlantic and in the Beturia region of <b>Extremadura</b> and Huelva.</blockquote><a href="/state/celtici">Celtici</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lusitanians lived between the Douro and the Tagus, in central Portugal and the neighbouring parts of <b>Extremadura</b>, emerging as a recognisable people in the second half of the first millennium BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusitanians">Lusitanians</a></li></ul></section>
